  "payload": {
    "evidence_contract": "judge_question:1",
    "question": {
      "dom_id": "question-121689",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14272746/assets/4d72a1caa88c1a0753f386c8aab657756497279e5bcd95eaaecaad6f1077c03a.png)\n\nThe height of a conical paper cup is $75\\text{ cm}$ and the radius of the base is $15\\text{ cm}.$ The cup is being filled with water so that the water level, $h$, rises at a rate of  $\\frac{1}{{h}^{2}}\\,\\text{cm/s}.$ At what rate is the volume of water in the cup increasing?",
      "worked_solution": "First, let's consider the dimensions of the cup. Let's call $H=75\\,\\text{cm}$ the height of the conical cup, and $R=15\\,\\text{cm}$ its radius at the top. These quantities are fixed and do not change with time.\n\nNow, let's consider the water level. We call $h$ the height of the water and $r$ the radius of the cone formed by the water. Both of these quantities, and the water volume $v,$ change with time. In particular, we know that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{1}{{h}^{2}}\\,\\text{cm}/\\text{s},\n$$\n\nand we want to know $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}.$\n\nWe need to find a relation between $v$ and the variable for which we know the rate of change, which is $h.$ This is given by the formula for the volume of a cone:\n\n$$\nv=\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {r}^{2}h\n$$\n\nBefore we differentiate the equation with respect to time, note that $r$ also appears in the formula. So, we need to simplify the formula by writing $r$ in terms of $h.$\n\nNote that the cones formed by the cup and the water must be similar. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{R}{H} \\\\ \\frac{r}{h} & =\\frac{15}{75} \\\\ r & =\\frac{h}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we can write the volume formula as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =\\frac{1}{3}\\pi {(\\frac{h}{5})}^{2}h \\\\ v & =\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow that we have an equation relating $v$ and $h,$ we differentiate with respect to time using the chain rule:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}v & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{75}\\pi {h}^{3}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}\\frac{\\text{d}h}{\\text{d}t} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi {h}^{2}(\\frac{1}{{h}^{2}}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{25}\\pi \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the volume of water is increasing at a constant rate of $\\frac{\\pi }{25}\\,\\,{\\text{cm}}^{3}\\text{/s}.$",
      "answer_fields_complete": true,
      "result": "Incorrect",
